---
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../config';

const contact = CONTACT_EMAIL.trim();
const updated = '30 August 2026';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What Evidence Stack collects: no cookies, no analytics, no third-party scripts. Only a newsletter email address, processed by Buttondown, and standard web server logs."
>
  <article>
    <div class="page-head">
      <div class="micro-label kicker">Last updated {updated}</div>
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Reading this site leaves nothing behind but an ordinary web server log line. There are
        no cookies, no analytics, no tracking pixels and no third-party scripts — pages, fonts
        and data all come from this domain. The only personal data collected on purpose is an
        email address, and only if you type one into the newsletter form.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
      <p>
        No cookies are set, and nothing is stored in your browser. No analytics or measurement
        service runs on any page. No content is loaded from another company's servers: the
        typefaces are served from this domain rather than from a font CDN, and there are no
        embeds, share buttons or ad tags. Nothing on this site is sold or handed to anyone for
        advertising, and there is no profiling of any kind.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The newsletter</h2>
      <p>
        The footer form submits directly to <b>Buttondown</b>, a newsletter service operated
        from the United States. Submitting it sends Buttondown your email address, along with
        the details any web request carries — your IP address, browser user agent and the time
        — plus whatever delivery records the service keeps for mail it sends. Buttondown is our
        processor: it stores and sends on our instructions and for no other purpose.
      </p>
      <p>
        The address is used for one thing: an email when a claim's grade moves. No digests, no
        news feed, no other mailings, and the list is never shared, sold or rented. The legal
        basis is your consent, given by submitting the form and withdrawn at any time — every
        email carries an unsubscribe link, and unsubscribing stops the mailing. Ask, and the
        address is deleted outright rather than only suppressed.
      </p>
      <div class="pending">
        <div class="micro-label pending-label">TO BE CONFIRMED</div>
        <p>
          The lawful transfer mechanism for sending personal data to a US processor is not yet
          stated here. It is being confirmed and will be named on this page.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Server logs</h2>
      <p>
        The web server records ordinary access logs: IP address, timestamp, requested URL,
        response status, browser user agent and referring page. These exist to keep the site
        running and to spot abuse, and they are not linked to any newsletter address or used to
        build a profile.
      </p>
      <div class="pending">
        <div class="micro-label pending-label">TO BE CONFIRMED</div>
        <p>
          The hosting provider and how long these logs are kept are not yet stated here.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How long anything is kept</h2>
      <p>
        A newsletter address is kept until you unsubscribe or ask for it to be deleted, and no
        separate copy of the list is kept outside Buttondown. Server log retention is the
        open question above. Everything else — the register itself — is public research
        content and contains no personal data about readers.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under the GDPR you can ask what data is held about you, have it corrected or deleted,
        object to or restrict its use, receive a copy of it, and withdraw consent at any time.
        For the newsletter, unsubscribing withdraws consent and stops the mailing; the rest
        work by asking, and are done rather than argued with. You can also complain to a data
        protection supervisory authority.
      </p>
      <div class="pending">
        <div class="micro-label pending-label">TO BE CONFIRMED</div>
        <p>
          The supervisory authority to complain to depends on where the operator is
          established. It will be named here once the operator record on the
          <a href="/about/">about page</a> is complete.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Who is responsible, and how to reach them</h2>
      {contact ? (
        <p>
          Data protection requests go to <a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a>, the same
          address as corrections. The operator record is on the
          <a href="/about/">about page</a>.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          The controller is the operator of this site, in the European Union. The operator's
          legal name and a contact address are not published yet — which means there is
          currently no way to send a request, and this notice is incomplete until there is.
          Both will appear here and on the <a href="/about/">about page</a>. Until then, the
          unsubscribe link in any email remains the working route for removing an address.
        </p>
      )}
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If what the site collects changes, this page changes with it and the date at the top
        moves. The site's full edit history, this page included, is kept in version control.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  article { max-width: 720px; padding: 32px 0 48px; }
  .kicker { color: var(--green-text); letter-spacing: 0.14em; margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .page-head h1 { font-size: 28px; font-weight: 800; margin: 0 0 12px; }
  .lede { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--body); text-wrap: pretty; }
  section { margin-top: 32px; }
  h2 { font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 10px; }
  p { font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--body); margin: 0 0 10px; }
  .pending {
    margin-top: 14px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--amber);
    background: var(--paper);
    padding: 12px 16px;
  }
  .pending-label { color: var(--amber); margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .pending p { font-size: 13px; margin: 0; }
</style>
